WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.000
A model starts out wrong. The only question is which way to move.

00:05.000 --> 00:12.000
It cannot see the whole curve, only the ground under its feet.

02:10.000 --> 02:16.000
Picture the error as a bowl. The bottom is the answer we want.

02:16.000 --> 02:24.000
Wherever the weight sits, the floor slopes one way or the other.

04:30.000 --> 04:38.000
The slope at a point is just how fast the error changes as the weight moves.

04:38.000 --> 04:45.000
For this bowl it is two times the distance from the bottom.

07:40.000 --> 07:48.000
Three steps on paper. Start at 4.5, step size 0.3.

07:48.000 --> 07:56.000
Each step takes away 0.6 of the distance, so it goes 4.5, 1.8, 0.72, 0.29.

10:20.000 --> 10:28.000
Too small a step and the walk takes forever. Too big and it overshoots.

10:28.000 --> 10:36.000
At step size one it lands on the other side, the same distance out, every time.

12:30.000 --> 12:38.000
Every step costs a pass over the training data to measure the slope.

12:38.000 --> 12:45.000
That is why real training takes so long, and why the step size matters so much.
